# Apple / iCloud Mail provider

Research date: 2026-09-02
Status: app-specific-password IMAP/SMTP onboarding implemented and narrowly qualified
with an authorized iCloud account (September 2–3, 2026), including scoped desktop
browser acceptance. Apple OAuth implementation remains deferred.

See [the method-by-method IMAP review](imap-provider-review.md) for evidence,
server-dependent capabilities, retained QA artifacts and remaining limitations.

This note concerns **iCloud Mail**, including eligible `icloud.com`, `me.com`,
and `mac.com` accounts. It does not mean the Apple Mail application, which can
display accounts hosted by other providers. Recheck the linked documentation
before implementation: authorization availability and enrollment may change.

## Recommendation

Keep Apple's official **iCloud OAuth 2.0 developer-interest route** as future work. Real
mailbox authorization exists, but public specifications and automatic enrollment
sufficient to implement Superlocal's connection have not been established.

Use **IMAP/SMTP with an app-specific password** for the implemented connection UI.
The limited iCloud qualification is documented separately; it is not blanket
production-readiness or compatibility with every IMAP extension/Apple identity.

OAuth and IMAP are not competing alternatives: OAuth handles authorization;
IMAP handles mailbox access. Apple's supported token transport still needs to
be confirmed. Do not assume that an approved OAuth grant necessarily uses IMAP.

## What the research established

1. **Apple supports genuine third-party mailbox authorization.** Its
   [October 7, 2025 guidance](https://support.apple.com/en-us/121539) describes
   entering an Apple Account address and selecting Allow in supported apps.
   Access is revoked under Apple Account > Sign-In and Security > Account Data
   Sharing > Remove access. Unsupported apps can use app-specific passwords.
2. **Developers can express interest.** In
   [Developer Forums thread 825772](https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/825772),
   an Apple DTS response in May 2026 directs an independent developer to
   [Express interest in iCloud OAuth 2.0](https://developer.apple.com/contact/request/icloud-oauth2/).
   Research stopped at the sign-in boundary. The intake establishes an
   Apple-mediated application route, not automatic acceptance or confirmed
   eligibility for Superlocal.
3. **Outlook demonstrates a deployed OAuth integration.** Microsoft's
   [October 30, 2025 announcement](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/outlook/it%E2%80%99s-now-easier-to-access-icloud-mail-calendar-and-contacts-in-outlook/4465707)
   covers iCloud Mail, Calendar, and Contacts in new Outlook for Windows,
   Outlook for Mac, and Outlook for iOS/Android. It does not establish a public
   registration process for other applications or include classic Outlook in
   that new flow.
4. **Ordinary Sign in with Apple is not mailbox authorization.** Its
   [identity-login documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/signinwithapple/authenticating-users-with-sign-in-with-apple)
   is not an iCloud Mail grant specification. Do not repurpose its scopes,
   endpoints, another application's registration, or private iCloud web cookies.
5. **An aggregator's OAuth can conceal an app-password upstream connection.**
   For example, [Nylas's iCloud guide](https://developer.nylas.com/docs/provider-guides/icloud/)
   requires a manually generated app-specific password, including its hosted
   authentication flow. A grant to an aggregator is not proof of Apple-issued
   mailbox OAuth.

## Questions Apple must answer before OAuth implementation

- Enrollment eligibility, approval requirements, and support for Superlocal's
  portable local-host/desktop architecture, including regional restrictions.
- Mail-only permissions, available operations, and whether mail access uses
  IMAP/SMTP, another API, or another transport. XOAUTH2/OAUTHBEARER support is
  not established by the existence of OAuth authorization alone.
- Authorization/token endpoints, scopes, redirect rules, PKCE, and client
  authentication requirements. No iCloud-mail-specific values were verified.
- Access-token lifetime, refresh/rotation, revocation notifications, and the
  effect of Apple Account password changes on OAuth grants.
- Stable account/mailbox/message identities, alias discovery, authorized From
  identities, and migration from an existing password connection.

Absence of public details in this research is not proof that the integration
is impossible or Microsoft-exclusive. Obtain Apple's documentation rather
than implementing against guessed or reverse-engineered endpoints.

## Documented password fallback

Apple's [server settings, February 3, 2026](https://support.apple.com/en-us/102525):

| Service | Server | Secure connection | Credentials |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Incoming mail | `imap.mail.me.com:993` | Implicit TLS; validate certificates | iCloud mailbox username and app-specific password |
| Outgoing mail | `smtp.mail.me.com:587` | Require STARTTLS; validate certificates | Full iCloud Mail address and the same app-specific password |

Apple documents the IMAP username as usually the mailbox's local part, with
the full address as an alternative. SMTP requires the full iCloud Mail
address. POP is unsupported. The server-settings page does not specify a
particular SASL password mechanism; qualify actual protocol behavior without
downgrading encryption.

### Credential lifecycle and onboarding

- [Apple's app-password guidance](https://support.apple.com/en-us/102654)
  requires two-factor authentication and permits up to 25 active passwords.
  These are reusable credentials, not one-time codes. No fixed expiry,
  refresh protocol, or read-only/folder-level/send-only scope controls are
  documented by this workflow.
- Request a dedicated, clearly labeled Superlocal app password, never the
  ordinary Apple Account password, MFA codes, or disabling two-factor
  authentication. Distinguish the iCloud mailbox address from an Apple Account
  login that might itself be an address at another provider.
- Submit credentials only to the authenticated host, clear transient UI state,
  and store them encrypted through the SDK. Do not persist them in browser
  storage, URLs, logs, telemetry, or deployment configuration.
- Individual passwords or all passwords can be revoked. Changing or resetting
  the primary Apple Account password revokes every app-specific password.
  Authentication failures require reconnection; outages, TLS failures, and
  quota errors need distinct handling and bounded retries.
- Disconnect must close sessions and remove local credentials. Explain that
  Apple-side password revocation is a separate user action; local deletion
  alone does not revoke Apple's credential.

## Superlocal integration boundaries

| Layer | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| Client | Connection/settings UI, capabilities, and status; no provider tokens or persisted passwords |
| Application host | Onboarding, Apple client registration/consent if approved, refresh/revocation coordination, and trusted endpoint configuration |
| Inbox SDK | Opaque encrypted owner/connection credentials, normalized contracts, storage, synchronization/workflow mechanics, and API behavior |
| Provider adapter | IMAP/SMTP or approved Apple protocol behavior, upstream translation, and evidenced capabilities |

Inbox splits remain client-owned views. Adding Apple support must not turn
Important/Other classification into provider label, folder, or flag changes.

### Implemented subset and remaining qualification

`packages/inbox-sdk/server/sdk/imap.ts` has separate IMAP/SMTP password or
access-token inputs and mandatory verified TLS. Native message identifiers include
account, mailbox path, UIDVALIDITY and UID. The host owns the fixed iCloud endpoints
and approved generic presets; the SDK stores credentials encrypted. The adapter
continues to advertise native `drafts: false`, `scheduledSend: false`, and
`pushNotifications: false`; the SDK implements its own drafts/scheduling/undo.

Authorized native-peer and live SDK checks covered the subset in the linked matrix.
Do not infer untested behavior from these code facts. Remaining or deliberately
bounded qualification includes:

1. Authentication, certificate validation, password revocation, reconnects,
   bounded retries, and connection cleanup.
2. Full-history/other-server synchronization, IDLE behavior and reconciliation,
   folders/special-use attributes, flags, moves/expunges, and UIDVALIDITY
   changes. Do not assume QRESYNC, CONDSTORE, UIDPLUS, or Apple push support.
3. Other-server SMTP delivery and Sent-copy policy. The qualified iCloud account
   did not save its SMTP probe to Sent automatically, so its preset appends once.
   The production reply's MIME/CIDs, RFC parent and exactly one Sent copy were
   checked. SMTP acceptance alone still does not prove Sent-folder persistence.
4. Primary addresses, eligible legacy addresses, aliases, custom domains, and
   verified From identities. Separate login identity from sending identity.
5. Stable message/source identity, threading, restart/recovery, and any
   password-to-OAuth migration, without duplicating previously synchronized mail.

## Address and service limitations

- [Eligible legacy addresses](https://support.apple.com/en-us/118230) and
  [iCloud aliases](https://support.apple.com/guide/icloud/what-are-email-aliases-in-icloud-mail-mm074af79454/icloud)
  are not automatically separate accounts/mailboxes. Apple ordinarily permits
  up to three aliases; qualify sending rather than assuming every alias is a
  valid login.
- [Custom email domains](https://support.apple.com/en-us/102540) require iCloud+.
  [Hide My Email](https://support.apple.com/guide/icloud/use-hide-my-email-in-mail-mmca4d729d79/icloud)
  provides forwarding/privacy addresses, not independent IMAP accounts. Do not
  promise arbitrary SMTP send-as or address creation/management through IMAP.
- Apple's [published mail limits](https://support.apple.com/en-us/102198)
  list 1,000 messages/day, 1,000 recipients/day, 500 recipients/message, and
  20 MB messages. Mail Drop's separate 5 GB facility is not a generic SMTP
  attachment allowance. Recheck limits before implementation.
- Full shared iCloud storage prevents sending/receiving. No authoritative
  concurrent-IMAP-connection or detailed synchronization-rate limit was found.
  Do not infer unlimited access or invent a provider-specific cap.
- IMAP access does not imply Apple Mail scheduling, undo-send, categorization,
  privacy features, or complete parity with Apple's native clients.

## Deferred next steps

1. Open [Apple's OAuth interest form](https://developer.apple.com/contact/request/icloud-oauth2/)
   and, with authorization, describe Superlocal's local-host architecture and
   mail-only requirements. Obtain eligibility and technical documentation.
2. Maintain the implemented app-specific-password path while evaluating Apple's
   response; do not expose an OAuth
   option before it is actually usable.
3. Complete coordinating-session browser/integration acceptance of the implemented
   onboarding without weakening encrypted credentials or secure transport.
4. Obtain separate authorization before expanding live qualification to other
   accounts, aliases, custom domains, irreversible deletion or a main-runtime import.
5. If migrating an existing connection to OAuth, preserve connection identity
   and provider namespace. Replace credentials in place when transport/native
   identities remain compatible. Otherwise reconcile explicitly: RFC
   Message-ID is useful evidence, not a guaranteed globally unique replacement
   key. Revoke the old app password only after the new connection succeeds,
   as Apple's authorization guidance recommends.

The original OAuth research did not change provider state or submit an Apple
application. Subsequent IMAP qualification was separately authorized and isolated;
it is recorded in `imap-provider-review.md`. This note does not grant additional
account access, application submission, password revocation or cleanup permission.
